{
  "id": "80827",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Elementary Reactions",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Earlier in this unit, we stressed that the rate law for a chemical reaction cannot be reliably predicted from the coefficients of the balanced chemical equation. However, unlike the balanced equation representing an overall reaction, the equation for an elementary step is an explicit representation of the chemical change taking place. The reactant(s) in an elementary step’s equation undergo only the bond-breaking and/or bond-making events depicted to yield the product(s). For this reason, the rate law for an elementary step may be derived directly from the balanced chemical equation describing the reaction. This is not the case for most chemical reaction equations, which do not precisely describe what occurs on a molecular level and for which rate laws may be reliably determined only by experimentation."
              }
            ],
            "id": "781biw6ja4sart0",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "yytc9snie3nzms6",
                "text": "Unimolecular Elementary Steps"
              }
            ],
            "id": "sg4g9x8usczgufs",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The number of reactant species (atoms, molecules, or ions) defines the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "molecularity"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The number of reactant species involved in an elementary step."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3409656871",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1986980775",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of an elementary step. For example, a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "unimolecular step"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An elementary step involving a single reactant species."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "661711847",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1160863947",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " involves the rearrangement of a single reactant species to produce one or more molecules of product:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8ea58lruct19u0t",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "zivoq5umiyjgzlp",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "rmrjuuub8hnal9b",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm A\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{products}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "An elementary reaction occurs exactly as written, so as the concentration of A increases, the reaction rate increases proportionally. Therefore, the rate law for a unimolecular step is:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ynrgr90yygxddde",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "l2uch5rn91krg25",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3gckw985p30661n",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Rate}\\;\\;=\\;\\;k\\lbrack\\mathrm A\\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A unimolecular step may be one of several elementary steps in a complex mechanism. For example, the first step in the decomposition of ozone has the following reaction equation and rate law:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ui065midq94tsjv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "u3qfeimsev1i0vg",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "gwfnxok3rnb7vj5",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm O}_3\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm O}_2\\;+\\;\\mathrm O\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; \\mathrm{Rate}\\;=\\;k\\left[{\\mathrm O}_3\\right]",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "However, some unimolecular steps may have only a single reaction in the reaction mechanism. (In other words, an elementary step can also be an overall reaction in some cases.) For example, the gas-phase decomposition of cyclobutane, C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": ", to ethylene, C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ", occurs via a unimolecular, single-step mechanism:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "m55l4th7fqvqeeu",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "In this figure, structural formulas are used to illustrate a chemical reaction. On the left, a structural formula for cyclobutane \n                    is shown. This structure is composed of 4 C atoms connected with single bonds in a square shape. Each C atom is bonded to two other C \n                    atoms in the structure, leaving two bonds for H atoms pointing outward above, below, left, and right. An arrow points right to two \n                    identical ethane molecules with a plus symbol between them. Each of these molecules contains two C atoms connected with a double bond \n                    oriented vertically between them. The C atom at the top of these molecules has H atoms bonded above to the right and left. Similarly, \n                    the lower C atom has two H atoms bonded below to the right and left.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "tei8zjhk0hos8gr",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "7mw4gzfdrot150g",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/3e/3ea7300495b825939fe598abecd669df/CNX_Chem_12_06_CyclobD_img.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "500"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For these unimolecular steps to occur, the primary requirement is that reactant molecules have the capacity to separate into parts (products) without interacting with another substance."
              }
            ],
            "id": "op9otce07ba4p8l",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Chemical bonds do not simply fall apart during chemical reactions. Energy is required to break chemical bonds. The activation energy for the decomposition of C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": ", for example, is 261 kJ per mole. This means that it requires 261 kilojoules to distort one mole of these molecules into activated complexes that decompose into products:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5w5jer192cwcnyk",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "In this figure, structural formulas are used to illustrate a chemical reaction, including an intermediate step. On the left, a \n                    structural formula for cyclobutane is shown. This structure is composed of 4 C atoms connected with single bonds in a square shape. \n                    Each C atom is bonded to two other C atoms in the structure, leaving two bonds for H atoms pointing outward above, below, left, and right. \n                    This structure is labeled, “Cyclohexane.” An arrow points right to a similar structure which has the upper and lower bonds replaced by \n                    rows of 4 dots. Similarly, columns of 3 dots appear just inside the line segments indicating the vertically oriented single bonds in the \n                    structure. The label “Activated complex” appears beneath this structure. A second arrow points right to two identical ethane molecules \n                    with a plus symbol between them. Each of these molecules contains two C atoms connected with a double bond oriented vertically between \n                    them. The C atom at the top of these molecules has H atoms bonded above to the right and left. Similarly, the lower C atom has two H \n                    atoms bonded below to the right and left. Below these two molecules appears the label “Ethylene.”",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "e75607d2re8us05",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "u618bho9ei30uaf",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/c1/c1d6c8c2400c8ea6f63d695971166442/CNX_Chem_12_06_CyclobDMec_img.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In a sample of C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": ", a few of the rapidly moving C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": " molecules collide with other rapidly moving molecules and pick up additional energy. When the C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": " molecules gain enough energy, they can transform into an activated complex, and the formation of ethylene molecules can occur. In effect, a particularly energetic collision knocks a C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": " molecule into the geometry of the activated complex. However, only a small fraction of gas molecules travel at sufficiently high speeds with large enough kinetic energies to accomplish this. Hence, at any given moment, only a few molecules pick up enough energy from collisions to react."
              }
            ],
            "id": "sbaj0z6wv8e6c1t",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The rate of decomposition of C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": " is directly proportional to its concentration. Doubling the concentration of C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": " in a sample gives twice as many molecules per liter. Although the fraction of molecules with enough energy to react remains the same, the total number of such molecules is twice as great. Consequently, there is twice as much C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "8"
              },
              {
                "text": " per liter, and the reaction rate is twice as fast:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "d7nxo8pammzijow",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "w2gf2lwoqsj5w9w",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "jou94k3vqla3rtd",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Rate}\\;=\\;-\\frac{\\Delta\\lbrack{\\mathrm C}_4{\\mathrm H}_8\\rbrack}{\\Delta t}\\;=\\;k\\lbrack{\\mathrm C}_4{\\mathrm H}_8 \\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A similar relationship applies to any unimolecular elementary step; the reaction rate is directly proportional to the concentration of the reactant, and the reaction exhibits first-order behavior. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "o6z27uwhff14l1n",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "9xwx7ffbpdmbo50",
                "text": "Bimolecular Elementary Steps"
              }
            ],
            "id": "jsu3ohhmbljkmmo",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The collision and combination of two molecules or atoms to form an activated complex in an elementary step is called a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "bimolecular step"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An elementary step involving two reactant species."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2895645516",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2488230179",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". There are two types of bimolecular elementary steps:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "y9myppvrh26fjzi",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "vv8ow6es2p78c5c",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "8t1hi6zy2kspjs5",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\begin{array}{c}\\mathrm A\\;+\\;\\mathrm B\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{products}\\\\\\mathrm{and}\\\\2\\;\\mathrm A\\;\\;\\longrightarrow \\;\\;\\mathrm{products}\\end{array}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For the first type, in which the two reactant molecules are different, the rate law is first order in A and first order in B:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "w7i35xjy5ag9lkl",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "rq4wquyksp057ol",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "m9l01h5kddaqz7c",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Rate}\\;=\\;k\\lbrack\\mathrm A\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm B\\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "For the second type, in which two identical molecules collide and react, the rate law is second order in A:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "xeo35wfxs4nwdr9",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "nmzke30d08livvg",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "r6xwio4l6nor2qt",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Rate}\\;=\\;k\\lbrack\\mathrm A\\rbrack\\lbrack\\mathrm A\\rbrack\\;=\\;k\\lbrack\\mathrm A\\rbrack^2",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Some chemical reactions have mechanisms that consist of a single bimolecular elementary step. One example is the reaction of nitrogen dioxide with carbon monoxide at temperatures above 500 K:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5b5392slf0gbs1d",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "rdgknlj1rm0tntb",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "oo59athja0gh7qc",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm{NO}}_2(g)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{CO}(g)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{NO}(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm{CO}}_2(g)\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; \\mathrm{Rate}\\;=\\;k\\left[{\\mathrm{NO}}_2\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{CO}\\right]",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "alt": "This figure provides an illustration of a reaction between an N O 2 molecule and a C O 2 molecule using space filling models. N atoms \n                    are shown as blue spheres, O atoms are shown as red spheres and C atoms are shown as black spheres. On the left, an N O 2 molecule is shown \n                    as a blue sphere in between two red spheres. It is next to a C O molecule shown as a black sphere next to a red sphere. The rightmost O in \n                    the N O 2 is near the C in the C O. An arrow points right to a similar structure in which the two molecules appear pushed together, so the \n                    rightmost red sphere of the N O 2 is touching the black sphere of the C O. Below appears the label, “Transition state.” Following another \n                    arrow pointing to the right, a red sphere touching a blue sphere is on the left and a black sphere in between two red spheres is on the \n                    left.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The probable mechanism for the reaction between NO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and CO to yield NO and CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " at temperatures above 500 K. Note that this reaction occurs by a different mechanism at temperatures below 500 K."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "fswgo83cebzudih",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "b4hjq3armay3spp",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/a1/a15593d3cf5c78397529563717139d6d/CNX_Chem_12_06_BimoElRe.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Bimolecular elementary steps may also be involved as steps in a multistep reaction mechanism. For example, the following is a bimolecular elementary step—and its associated rate law—that is the second step in the decomposition of ozone:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "np8p234ki0vi4r3",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "hlqzwvqi8zllnqa",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "xjy9o235chrszru",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "2\\;\\mathrm{NO}\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_2\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;2\\;{\\mathrm{NO}}_2",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "c4yx7p47w6li2ta",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "u0ori630i1o7hh6",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Rate}\\;=\\;k\\lbrack\\mathrm{NO}\\rbrack^2\\lbrack{\\mathrm O}_2\\rbrack",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "4gp2l5guofpatlf",
                "text": "Termolecular Elementary Steps"
              }
            ],
            "id": "tpurvmuod0r8mwm",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "An elementary "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "termolecular step"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An elementary step involving three reactant species."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3213988450",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2761631058",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " involves the simultaneous collision of three atoms, molecules, or ions. Termolecular elementary steps are very uncommon because the probability of three particles colliding simultaneously is less than one one-thousandth of the probability of two particles colliding. There are, however, a few established termolecular elementary steps. For example, the reaction of nitrogen monoxide with oxygen appears to involve a termolecular step:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "5dv207akb887156",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "btsx7li97vphja1",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "uca750y9ws1tpdv",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\begin{array}{l}2\\;\\mathrm{NO}\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_2\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;2\\;{\\mathrm{NO}}_2\\\\\\mathrm{Rate}\\;=\\;k\\lbrack\\mathrm{NO} \\rbrack^2\\lbrack{\\mathrm O}_2\\rbrack\\end{array}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Rate laws for unimolecular, bimolecular, and termolecular elementary steps are summarized in the following table. Note that because an elementary step occurs in one step, its rate law can be determined directly from the reaction stoichiometry (the reaction coefficients are the rate orders). This holds true only for an elementary step, not for an overall reaction. If a reaction's known (experimental) rate law does not correspond to the molecularity of the overall reaction, the reaction "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "cannot"
              },
              {
                "text": " occur by a single elementary step and must have a multistep mechanism. As we will see next, if the known rate law matches the molecularity of the overall reaction, we can conclude only that the reaction "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "might"
              },
              {
                "text": " occur by a single elementary step."
              }
            ],
            "id": "djwc828qv7dz70m",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "7nljc9zhco9qahc",
                        "text": "Elementary Step"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "bmzzzdyyk9h63yq",
                    "type": "th"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "3268tbhwy6osxla",
                        "text": "Molecularity"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "yrqfi4wn5s8on2d",
                    "type": "th"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "0bxgrymybl3q5x7",
                        "text": "Rate Law"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "a87u1kap1b3gqqc",
                    "type": "th"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "tvm1aca31jkfpni",
                "type": "tr"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "htwhjldxlptxm7i",
                        "text": "A → products"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "80per2e4yn83dbb",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "gfmg92so9i5vhdy",
                        "text": "unimolecular"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "lrz4bxeu4izzqxb",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "wp6i58319xhrqcu",
                        "text": "Rate = "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "id": "u4wyji0h8ryes31",
                        "text": "k"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "4xmuanilxcv0lne",
                        "text": "[A]"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "txr3mxgxmpuzma7",
                    "type": "td"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "zvw992bqyluynxb",
                "type": "tr"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "0y73bsp5yth0vgx",
                        "text": "2 A → products"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "ce06mbel7qk3akc",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "s1kl6qixciyyquh",
                        "text": "bimolecular"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "q5uu20lvmi9t869",
                    "type": "td"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "id": "etyw7qgd8zsh3pu",
                        "text": "Rate = "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "id": "d93h940zw6m2iju",
                        "text": "k"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "097y95qa7y2qv7a",
                        "text": "[A]"
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "zqmsmd2sgtnnd2e",
